WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.741
On Day 55, Docker handed each log line straight to Fluent Bit.

2
00:00:06.741 --> 00:00:08.631
Kubernetes works differently.

3
00:00:08.631 --> 00:00:14.432
Here is one node, with hello-devops running on it, and Elasticsearch outside the cluster.

4
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:16.643
The app just prints to stdout.

5
00:00:16.643 --> 00:00:20.193
The container runtime on the node catches every line.

6
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.192
It writes each container's output to a file.

7
00:00:23.192 --> 00:00:31.187
The kubelet links those files into one folder, and the file name says who wrote it: the pod, the namespace, and the container.

8
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.886
Each line in that file is wrapped by the runtime.

9
00:00:34.886 --> 00:00:38.917
A timestamp, the stream, a flag, and then the app's own message.

10
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:45.433
The flag is F for a full line, or P for a partial one, when the runtime splits a very long line.

11
00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:50.257
So the job is: read every file in that folder, on every node.

12
00:00:50.257 --> 00:00:52.334
A DaemonSet does exactly that.

13
00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:55.035
It runs one copy of a pod on each node.

14
00:00:55.267 --> 00:01:01.889
A hostPath volume shows the node's own /var/log folder inside the Fluent Bit container.

15
00:01:01.889 --> 00:01:03.792
So it can read the files.

16
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.324
Your kind cluster has three nodes, so three copies.

17
00:01:07.324 --> 00:01:11.885
Add a node, and Kubernetes starts another Fluent Bit there, by itself.

18
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.184
Why not a log sidecar in every app pod?

19
00:01:15.184 --> 00:01:15.803
It works.

20
00:01:15.803 --> 00:01:23.165
But forty app pods would mean forty shippers, each with its own memory and config, instead of one per node.

21
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:27.780
The tail input follows each file, the way tail -f does.

22
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:35.513
With the multiline parser set to docker and cri, it unwraps this format, and joins P pieces back together.

23
00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:41.073
In the lab test, a line split into a P and an F part came out as one record.

24
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.612
A small database stores how far each file was read.

25
00:01:44.612 --> 00:01:48.055
So a restarted Fluent Bit continues where it stopped.

26
00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:52.124
But a bare log line does not say which pod wrote it.

27
00:01:52.124 --> 00:01:55.131
How would anyone find it later, among thousands?

28
00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:57.355
The kubernetes filter adds that.

29
00:01:57.355 --> 00:02:02.408
The pod name, the namespace and the container come from the file name, for free.

30
00:02:02.633 --> 00:02:11.365
For the labels, it asks the Kubernetes API about the pod, then keeps the answer in a cache, so it does not ask again for every line.

31
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:14.826
Then merge_log checks whether the message is JSON.

32
00:02:14.826 --> 00:02:17.303
If it is, its keys become real fields.

33
00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:22.061
And keep_log off drops the original string, so you do not store it twice.

34
00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:26.135
The finished record goes to Elasticsearch, outside the cluster.

35
00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:30.410
In Kibana, those fields become the filters you use every day.

36
00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.332
Show me only namespace hello.

37
00:02:32.533 --> 00:02:34.227
One warning about scale.

38
00:02:34.227 --> 00:02:38.816
A thousand nodes asking the API server about pods adds real load.

39
00:02:38.816 --> 00:02:43.263
The filter's use_kubelet option asks the local kubelet instead.

40
00:02:43.967 --> 00:02:46.378
Every node runs its own Fluent Bit.

41
00:02:46.378 --> 00:02:54.988
It tails the node's container log files, adds labels from the Kubernetes API, and ships to Elasticsearch outside the cluster.
